## 4. Project management — CPMAI × Agile

The project plan combines AI lifecycle governance (business understanding → data understanding → preparation → modeling → evaluation → operationalization) with four time-boxed sprints:

| Sprint | Core focus | Outputs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 — Weeks 2–3 | Business challenge and data discovery | AI Canvas, business analysis, data inventory, mockups |
| 2 — Weeks 4–5 | Data preparation and baseline models | EDA, preprocessing, baseline and initial metrics |
| 3 — Weeks 6–7 | Model improvement and validation | Final-model comparison, SHAP, business case, deployment plan |
| 4 — Weeks 8–9 | MVP, packaging and handoff | Interactive demo, presentation and final report |

The **10-week plan** includes initial setup and wrap-up outside the four 2-week sprint blocks. See the [project management hub](docs/project-management/README.md) for traceable PM documents, planned OKRs, stakeholder communication, risk controls and evidence-quality notes.

## 5. Data & ML approach

- **Data:** A coursework sample of **500 multimodal trip records and 50 columns** was loaded in the notebook from `multimodal_trip_samples`. The original workbook `hcm_multimodal_dataset_v3.xlsx` is **not included** in the provided source files. See [Data README](data/README.md).
- **Target:** `recommended_total_cost_vnd` — cost prediction in VND; not a trip-duration or ETA target.
- **Inputs:** nine pre-trip fields: distance, traffic level, bus/metro availability, passenger count, time-of-day, walking distance to transit, pickup ETA and nearby driver distance.
- **Pipeline:** numeric/categorical preprocessing with imputation inside the scikit-learn pipeline (to reduce preprocessing leakage), model comparison and held-out split (80/20).
- **Baselines and candidates:** Dummy, Linear Regression, Ridge, Decision Tree, Random Forest.
- **Evaluation:** MAE, MAPE, RMSE and R²; explanatory analysis includes feature importance and SHAP.

| Model | MAE (VND) | MAPE | RMSE (VND) | R² |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| **RandomForest_Deep** | **495.68** | **6.74%** | **569.65** | **0.931** |
| Ridge | 562.58 | 6.93% | 860.35 | 0.843 |
| Linear Regression | 564.35 | 6.94% | 866.33 | 0.841 |
| Dummy | 784.17 | 8.54% | 2,173.43 | -0.000 |
| Decision Tree | 958.25 | 11.06% | 1,958.15 | 0.188 |

*Metrics transcribed from the saved model-comparison output of the supplied notebook; not rerun because the training data workbook was not supplied. Values reported by the notebook may differ from an evaluation on new real-world data.*

### Why Random Forest?

The project tested simple baselines against a tree ensemble because costs depend nonlinearly on distance, traffic conditions and mode availability. Random Forest obtained the lowest MAE in the saved comparison and supports feature-importance diagnostics. This alone does **not** establish performance under unseen cities, live fares, seasonal disruptions or routing constraints.

## 6. Business & success metrics

The team produced a modeled business evaluation workbook and **proposed** launch OKRs. These are scenario targets, **not validated Grab operational achievements**. Examples include: 20,000 beta participants, 15% shorter journeys, 99.5% uptime and partner API freshness within five minutes. See [OKRs](docs/project-management/Project_OKRs.pdf) and [business evaluation](docs/business/Business_Evaluation.xlsx).

## 7. Limitations & next steps

- Training workbook and fitted `model.pkl` were not among the uploaded deliverables; the notebook is provided as **archived project source**, not a one-command reproducible app.
- Some route, bus/metro, weather/traffic and time assumptions are approximations or simulated; the demo is not a verified production-grade city routing engine.
- Data represent a limited HCMC corridor and 2025 study context; validate address changes, stops, service patterns and coverage before deployment.
- Recheck model generalization with independent real-world fare and journey records, time-based splits, out-of-distribution tests, latency benchmarks and route feasibility checks.
- Replace random traffic inputs with authorized, timestamped feeds; model actual ETA separately; build a user-facing application and instrument adoption / satisfaction tests.
- The project closeout draft contains conflicting rollout/ROI statements and timeline dates versus the final report / plan; treat it as a **planning artifact**, not proof of a live launch. See [evidence notes](results/Evidence_and_Limitations.md).

